/-!
# A superoperator for the electromagnetic field

Constructs the **superoperator generated by the electromagnetic field strength** `F = dA`
(`PTSymmetricQFT.MaxwellFaraday.faraday`) — the adjoint (commutator) action `ad_F = [F, ·]` on the operator
algebra `Matrix (Fin 4) (Fin 4)`. Since `F^μ_ν` is antisymmetric (`faraday_antisymm`) it is a Lorentz
Lie-algebra element, and `ad_F` is the **infinitesimal Lorentz transformation the field generates** — the
electric part acting as boosts, the magnetic part as rotations.

The construction is identical in form to the Saveliev collision bracket `collisionStar = ad` of
`CollisionOperatorSl2.LinearBoltzmannOperator`, so the EM superoperator *inherits* its entire Lie structure (derivation /
Leibniz, the adjoint homomorphism `[ad_F, ad_G] = ad_{[F,G]}`, the Jacobi identity) from `collisionStar` —
and its Jacobi identity is the *same* one that serves as the BCJ **color** Jacobi
(`BCJDoubleCopy.SavelievLieColor`). The `ℂ`-Liouville generator `𝓛_F = −i[F, ·]` (Heisenberg time evolution) is
the `heisenbergGenerator` of `PTSymmetricQFT.LindbladSuperoperator` for the complexified field.

* **§A — the EM field adjoint superoperator** (`emFieldAdjoint`, `emFieldAdjoint_apply`,
  `emFieldAdjoint_eq_collisionStar`, `emFieldAdjoint_one`, `emFieldAdjoint_self`). `ad_F = mulLeft F −
  mulRight F`, `ad_F(X) = [F, X]`, `ad_F(1) = 0`, `ad_F(F) = 0`.
* **§B — the inherited Lie structure** (`emFieldAdjoint_leibniz`, `emFieldAdjoint_ad_hom`,
  `emFieldAdjoint_jacobi`). A derivation; the adjoint homomorphism `[ad_F, ad_G] = ad_{[F,G]}`; the Jacobi
  identity — all from `collisionStar`. The Jacobi is the BCJ color Jacobi.
* **§C — the field strength and the Heisenberg/Liouville generator** (`emFieldComplex`, `emLiouvillian`,
  `emLiouvillian_apply`, `faraday_emFieldAdjoint_self`). The actual `F = dA` generates the superoperator; the
  `ℂ` time-evolution generator `𝓛_F = −i[F, ·]` is `heisenbergGenerator` of the complexified field.
* **§D — Sachs-Schwebel complex Maxwell field** (`sachsRiemannSilbersteinField`,
  `sachsMaxwellInvariant_eq`, `sachsOutputSpectrum_eq`). The complex Maxwell field `G = H - iE` is a faithful
  packaging of the real electric and magnetic fields; its invariant is `E² - H² + 2i E·H`, and its mode
  power is linked to the existing spectral `outputSpectrum` API.
* **§E — Sachs-Schwebel spinor and matrix forms** (`sachsPhi1`, `sachsPhi2`, `sachsFieldMatrix`,
  `sachsSpinorPair_phi2_phi1`, `sachsFieldMatrix_det`, `sachsCurrentSpinorMatrix_det`,
  `sachsBispinor`, `sachsMatrixEquation_iff_columns`, `sachsWignerTimeReverse_sq`). This formalizes Eq.
  (4)'s two-spinor packaging, Eq. (5)'s four-spinor packaging, Eq. (9)'s `2×2` field matrix, the `iσ₂`
  bilinear invariant using the existing Pauli matrices, the source/current determinant `j²-ρ²`, and the
  Wigner operator `W = iσ₂K`.
* **§F — Poynting balance** (`sachsEnergyDensity`, `sachsPoyntingVector`, `sachsPoyntingBalance`,
  `sachsPoyntingBalance_energyRate`, `sachsConservationLaw_zero_source_iff`). This records Eq. (11)'s
  bilinear conservation-law source term and Eq. (12)'s energy-balance scalar form while reusing Mathlib's
  three-dimensional cross product for `E×H`.
* **§G — appendix covariance link** (`sachsSL2CSpinorAction`, `sachsSL2CAppendixCovariance`). This reuses
  the repository's `SL(2,ℂ) → SO⁺(1,3)` construction: the determinant of the self-adjoint Pauli matrix is
  preserved and the induced Lorentz transformation is proper and orthochronous.

## References

* The electromagnetic field strength `F_{μν} = ∂_μ A_ν − ∂_ν A_μ` as a Lorentz Lie-algebra element; the
  adjoint (Liouville/Heisenberg) action of an operator on the operator algebra.
* Repo carriers: `PTSymmetricQFT.MaxwellFaraday` (`faraday`, `faraday_antisymm`);
  `CollisionOperatorSl2.LinearBoltzmannOperator` / `CollisionOperatorSl2.CollisionModular` (`collisionStar`,
  `collisionStar_leibniz`, `collisionStar_ad_hom`, `collisionStar_jacobi`);
  `PTSymmetricQFT.LindbladSuperoperator` (`heisenbergGenerator`);
  `Schrodinger.SpectralDynamics` (`outputSpectrum`);
  `Relativity.PauliMatrices.Basic` (`pauliMatrix`);
  `Relativity.SL2C.Basic` (`toSelfAdjointMap_apply_det`, `toLorentzGroup_det_one`,
  `toLorentzGroup_isOrthochronous`); Mathlib `crossProduct`.
* M. Sachs and S. L. Schwebel, "On Covariant Formulations of the Maxwell-Lorentz Theory of
  Electromagnetism", Journal of Mathematical Physics 3, 843 (1962), DOI: 10.1063/1.1724297.


-/

set_option autoImplicit false

@[expose] public section

open scoped BigOperators Matrix MatrixGroups

namespace Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.Electromagnetic.EMFieldSuperoperator

open Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.PTSymmetricQFT.MaxwellFaraday
open Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.CollisionOperatorSl2.CollisionModular
open Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.CollisionOperatorSl2.LinearBoltzmannOperator
open Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.PTSymmetricQFT.LindbladSuperoperator
open Physlib.QuantumMechanics.Schrodinger.SpectralDynamics

/-- The operator algebra on which the EM field strength acts as a superoperator — real `4×4` matrices
(spacetime indices). -/
abbrev Mat := Matrix (Fin 4) (Fin 4) ℝ


/-- **The superoperator of the electromagnetic field strength** `ad_F = [F, ·] = mulLeft F − mulRight F`.
With `F^μ_ν` antisymmetric (a Lorentz generator), `ad_F` is the infinitesimal Lorentz transformation the
field generates. -/
noncomputable def emFieldAdjoint (F : Mat) : Mat →ₗ[ℝ] Mat :=
  LinearMap.mulLeft ℝ F - LinearMap.mulRight ℝ F

/-- **`ad_F(X) = [F, X]`** — the commutator action. -/
@[simp] lemma emFieldAdjoint_apply (F X : Mat) : emFieldAdjoint F X = F * X - X * F := by
  simp [emFieldAdjoint]

/-- **`ad_F` is the Saveliev `collisionStar`** (`= ad`) of the field strength — the EM superoperator is the
adjoint bracket, identifying it with the collision/Lie bracket of `CollisionOperatorSl2.LinearBoltzmannOperator`. -/
lemma emFieldAdjoint_eq_collisionStar (F X : Mat) : emFieldAdjoint F X = collisionStar F X := by
  rw [emFieldAdjoint_apply, collisionStar]

/-- **`ad_F(1) = 0`** — the field commutes with the identity. -/
@[simp] lemma emFieldAdjoint_one (F : Mat) : emFieldAdjoint F 1 = 0 := by
  simp [emFieldAdjoint_apply]

/-- **`ad_F(F) = 0`** — the field commutes with itself. -/
@[simp] lemma emFieldAdjoint_self (F : Mat) : emFieldAdjoint F F = 0 := by
  rw [emFieldAdjoint_eq_collisionStar, collisionStar_self]


/-- **`ad_F` is a derivation** `ad_F(XY) = ad_F(X)·Y + X·ad_F(Y)` — from `collisionStar_leibniz`. -/
lemma emFieldAdjoint_leibniz (F X Y : Mat) :
    emFieldAdjoint F (X * Y) = emFieldAdjoint F X * Y + X * emFieldAdjoint F Y := by
  simp only [emFieldAdjoint_eq_collisionStar]; exact collisionStar_leibniz F X Y

/-- **The adjoint homomorphism `[ad_F, ad_G] = ad_{[F,G]}`** — the EM field adjoints close into a Lie
algebra (the Lorentz algebra). From `collisionStar_ad_hom`. -/
lemma emFieldAdjoint_ad_hom (F G X : Mat) :
    emFieldAdjoint F (emFieldAdjoint G X) - emFieldAdjoint G (emFieldAdjoint F X)
      = emFieldAdjoint (emFieldAdjoint F G) X := by
  simp only [emFieldAdjoint_eq_collisionStar]; exact collisionStar_ad_hom F G X

/-- **The Jacobi identity** `[F,[G,X]] + [G,[X,F]] + [X,[F,G]] = 0` — the EM field adjoint's Jacobi, *the
same* identity that serves as the BCJ color Jacobi (`BCJDoubleCopy.SavelievLieColor`). From
`collisionStar_jacobi`. -/
lemma emFieldAdjoint_jacobi (F G X : Mat) :
    emFieldAdjoint F (emFieldAdjoint G X) + emFieldAdjoint G (emFieldAdjoint X F)
      + emFieldAdjoint X (emFieldAdjoint F G) = 0 := by
  simp only [emFieldAdjoint_eq_collisionStar]; exact collisionStar_jacobi F G X


/-- **The complexified electromagnetic field strength** `F_ℂ = dA` (as a complex matrix, for the unitary
time evolution). -/
noncomputable def emFieldComplex (k A : Fin 4 → ℝ) : Matrix (Fin 4) (Fin 4) ℂ :=
  (faraday k A).map (Complex.ofReal)

/-- **The EM Liouville / Heisenberg generator** `𝓛_F = −i[F, ·]` — the unitary time-evolution superoperator
generated by the field, the `heisenbergGenerator` of `PTSymmetricQFT.LindbladSuperoperator`. -/
noncomputable def emLiouvillian (F : Matrix (Fin 4) (Fin 4) ℂ) :
    Matrix (Fin 4) (Fin 4) ℂ →ₗ[ℂ] Matrix (Fin 4) (Fin 4) ℂ :=
  heisenbergGenerator F

/-- **`𝓛_F(Y) = −i[F, Y]`** — the Heisenberg equation of motion under the field. -/
lemma emLiouvillian_apply (F Y : Matrix (Fin 4) (Fin 4) ℂ) :
    emLiouvillian F Y = -Complex.I • (F * Y - Y * F) := by
  rw [emLiouvillian, heisenbergGenerator_apply]

/-- **The field strength `F = dA` commutes with itself under its own adjoint** `ad_F(F) = 0` — the
electromagnetic field generates a superoperator that annihilates the field itself. -/
@[simp] lemma faraday_emFieldAdjoint_self (k A : Fin 4 → ℝ) :
    emFieldAdjoint (faraday k A) (faraday k A) = 0 :=
  emFieldAdjoint_self _


/-! ## D. Sachs-Schwebel complex Maxwell field -/

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel complex Maxwell field** `G = H - iE`. This is the
Riemann-Silberstein packaging used by Sachs and Schwebel to write Maxwell-Lorentz
electromagnetism in a Dirac/Weyl-like first-order form. -/
def sachsRiemannSilbersteinField (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Fin 3 → ℂ :=
  fun k => (H k : ℂ) - Complex.I * (E k : ℂ)

/-- **Squared Euclidean norm of a real three-field** `V² = ∑ₖ Vₖ²`. -/
def sachsFieldNormSq (V : Fin 3 → ℝ) : ℝ := ∑ k, V k ^ 2

/-- **Euclidean dot product of two real three-fields** `E·H = ∑ₖ EₖHₖ`. -/
def sachsFieldDot (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : ℝ := ∑ k, E k * H k

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Maxwell invariant** obtained from `G = H - iE`.
With this sign convention, `-∑ₖ Gₖ² = E² - H² + 2i E·H`. -/
def sachsMaxwellInvariant (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : ℂ :=
  -∑ k, sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H k ^ 2

/-- The real part of the Sachs-Schwebel field `G = H - iE` recovers `H`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsRiemannSilbersteinField_re (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) (k : Fin 3) :
    (sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H k).re = H k := by
  simp [sachsRiemannSilbersteinField]

/-- The imaginary part of the Sachs-Schwebel field `G = H - iE` recovers `-E`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsRiemannSilbersteinField_im (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) (k : Fin 3) :
    (sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H k).im = -E k := by
  simp [sachsRiemannSilbersteinField]

/-- The pointwise quadratic Sachs-Schwebel identity
`-(H - iE)² = E² - H² + 2iEH`. -/
lemma sachsMaxwellInvariant_term (e h : ℝ) :
    -(((h : ℂ) - Complex.I * (e : ℂ)) ^ 2) =
      ((e ^ 2 - h ^ 2 : ℝ) : ℂ) + Complex.I * ((2 * e * h : ℝ) : ℂ) := by
  apply Complex.ext
  · simp only [pow_two, Complex.neg_re, Complex.sub_re, Complex.sub_im, Complex.mul_re,
      Complex.mul_im, Complex.I_re, Complex.I_im, Complex.ofReal_re, Complex.ofReal_im,
      zero_mul, one_mul, sub_zero, mul_zero, zero_sub, Complex.add_re]
    ring_nf
  · simp only [pow_two, Complex.neg_im, Complex.sub_re, Complex.sub_im, Complex.mul_re,
      Complex.mul_im, Complex.I_re, Complex.I_im, Complex.ofReal_re, Complex.ofReal_im,
      zero_mul, one_mul, sub_zero, mul_zero, zero_sub, Complex.add_im]
    ring_nf

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel invariant split.** The quadratic invariant of the complex
Maxwell field is exactly `E² - H² + 2i E·H`. -/
lemma sachsMaxwellInvariant_eq (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsMaxwellInvariant E H =
      ((sachsFieldNormSq E - sachsFieldNormSq H : ℝ) : ℂ) +
        Complex.I * ((2 * sachsFieldDot E H : ℝ) : ℂ) := by
  apply Complex.ext
  · unfold sachsMaxwellInvariant sachsFieldNormSq sachsFieldDot sachsRiemannSilbersteinField
    rw [← Finset.sum_neg_distrib]
    simp_rw [sachsMaxwellInvariant_term]
    simp [Finset.sum_sub_distrib]
  · unfold sachsMaxwellInvariant sachsFieldNormSq sachsFieldDot sachsRiemannSilbersteinField
    rw [← Finset.sum_neg_distrib]
    simp_rw [sachsMaxwellInvariant_term]
    simp [Finset.sum_add_distrib, Finset.mul_sum, sub_eq_add_neg]
    apply Finset.sum_congr rfl
    intro k _
    ring

/-- The real part of the Sachs-Schwebel invariant is the standard electromagnetic
invariant `E² - H²`. -/
lemma sachsMaxwellInvariant_re (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsMaxwellInvariant E H).re = sachsFieldNormSq E - sachsFieldNormSq H := by
  rw [sachsMaxwellInvariant_eq]
  simp

/-- The imaginary part of the Sachs-Schwebel invariant is `2 E·H`. -/
lemma sachsMaxwellInvariant_im (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsMaxwellInvariant E H).im = 2 * sachsFieldDot E H := by
  rw [sachsMaxwellInvariant_eq]
  simp [sachsFieldDot]

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel complex field is faithful: `G = 0` iff both real fields
vanish. -/
lemma sachsComplexField_zero_iff (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (∀ k, sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H k = 0) ↔ E = 0 ∧ H = 0 := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    constructor
    · funext k
      have hi := congrArg Complex.im (h k)
      simp [sachsRiemannSilbersteinField] at hi
      exact hi
    · funext k
      have hr := congrArg Complex.re (h k)
      simpa [sachsRiemannSilbersteinField] using hr
  · intro h
    rcases h with ⟨hE, hH⟩
    intro k
    simp [sachsRiemannSilbersteinField, hE, hH]

/-- **Sachs mode power.** For each component, the spectral power of the complex
Maxwell amplitude is `|Gₖ|² = Hₖ² + Eₖ²`. -/
lemma sachsComplexField_normSq (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) (k : Fin 3) :
    Complex.normSq (sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H k) = H k ^ 2 + E k ^ 2 := by
  simp [sachsRiemannSilbersteinField, Complex.normSq]
  ring

/-- The total Sachs-Schwebel complex-field power is the sum of magnetic and
electric squared norms. -/
lemma sachsComplexField_totalPower (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (∑ k, Complex.normSq (sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H k)) =
      sachsFieldNormSq H + sachsFieldNormSq E := by
  unfold sachsFieldNormSq
  simp [sachsComplexField_normSq, Finset.sum_add_distrib]

/-- A Sachs-Schwebel Maxwell mode uses the existing spectral `outputSpectrum`
definition: an input spectral power is multiplied by `|Gₖ|² = Hₖ² + Eₖ²`. -/
lemma sachsOutputSpectrum_eq (inputPower : ℝ) (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) (k : Fin 3) :
    outputSpectrum inputPower (sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H k) =
      inputPower * (H k ^ 2 + E k ^ 2) := by
  simp [outputSpectrum, sachsComplexField_normSq]

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel invariant vanishes for the zero electromagnetic field. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsMaxwellInvariant_zero :
    sachsMaxwellInvariant (fun _ : Fin 3 => 0) (fun _ : Fin 3 => 0) = 0 := by
  simp [sachsMaxwellInvariant, sachsRiemannSilbersteinField]


/-! ## E. Sachs-Schwebel spinor and matrix forms -/

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (4), first Maxwell spinor.** With `G = H - iE`,
`φ₁ = (G₃, G₁ + iG₂)`. The indices `0,1,2` correspond to the paper's
spatial labels `1,2,3`. -/
def sachsPhi1 (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Fin 2 → ℂ :=
  ![sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 2,
    sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 0 + Complex.I * sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 1]

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (4), second Maxwell spinor.** With `G = H - iE`,
`φ₂ = (-G₁ + iG₂, G₃)`. -/
def sachsPhi2 (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Fin 2 → ℂ :=
  ![-sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 0 + Complex.I * sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 1,
    sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 2]

/-- The first component of the first Sachs-Schwebel Maxwell spinor is `G₃`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsPhi1_zero (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsPhi1 E H 0 = sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 2 := by
  simp [sachsPhi1]

/-- The second component of the first Sachs-Schwebel Maxwell spinor is `G₁+iG₂`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsPhi1_one (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsPhi1 E H 1 =
      sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 0 + Complex.I * sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 1 := by
  simp [sachsPhi1]

/-- The first component of the second Sachs-Schwebel Maxwell spinor is `-G₁+iG₂`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsPhi2_zero (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsPhi2 E H 0 =
      -sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 0 + Complex.I * sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 1 := by
  simp [sachsPhi2]

/-- The second component of the second Sachs-Schwebel Maxwell spinor is `G₃`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsPhi2_one (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsPhi2 E H 1 = sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 2 := by
  simp [sachsPhi2]

/-- The `iσ₂` spinor metric used in the Sachs-Schwebel invariant bilinear. -/
def sachsSpinorMetricMatrix : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ :=
  Complex.I • PauliMatrix.pauliMatrix (Sum.inr (1 : Fin 3))

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel `iσ₂` metric is the standard antisymmetric matrix. -/
lemma sachsSpinorMetricMatrix_eq :
    sachsSpinorMetricMatrix = !![0, 1; -1, 0] := by
  ext i j
  fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;> simp [sachsSpinorMetricMatrix, PauliMatrix.pauliMatrix]

/-- The antisymmetric two-spinor pairing `uᵀ(iσ₂)v = u₀v₁-u₁v₀`. -/
def sachsSpinorPair (u v : Fin 2 → ℂ) : ℂ := u 0 * v 1 - u 1 * v 0

/-- The antisymmetric spinor pairing is anti-symmetric. -/
lemma sachsSpinorPair_swap (u v : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsSpinorPair v u = -sachsSpinorPair u v := by
  unfold sachsSpinorPair
  ring

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel spinor bilinear gives the electromagnetic invariant:
`φ₂ᵀ(iσ₂)φ₁ = E²-H²+2iE·H`. -/
lemma sachsSpinorPair_phi2_phi1 (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsSpinorPair (sachsPhi2 E H) (sachsPhi1 E H) = sachsMaxwellInvariant E H := by
  have hI2 : Complex.I ^ 2 = (-1 : ℂ) := by
    rw [pow_two, Complex.I_mul_I]
  unfold sachsSpinorPair sachsPhi1 sachsPhi2 sachsMaxwellInvariant
  simp [Fin.sum_univ_three]
  ring_nf
  rw [hI2]
  ring

/-- Reversing the order of the Sachs-Schwebel spinor bilinear flips the invariant sign. -/
lemma sachsSpinorPair_phi1_phi2 (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsSpinorPair (sachsPhi1 E H) (sachsPhi2 E H) = -sachsMaxwellInvariant E H := by
  rw [sachsSpinorPair_swap, sachsSpinorPair_phi2_phi1]

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (9), field matrix.** Its columns are the two spinors
`φ₁, φ₂`, so
`Φ = !![G₃, -G₁+iG₂; G₁+iG₂, G₃]`. -/
def sachsFieldMatrix (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ :=
  !![sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 2,
    -sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 0 + Complex.I * sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 1;
    sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 0 + Complex.I * sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 1,
    sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 2]

/-- The first column of the Sachs-Schwebel field matrix is `φ₁`. -/
lemma sachsFieldMatrix_col_zero (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (fun i => sachsFieldMatrix E H i 0) = sachsPhi1 E H := by
  funext i
  fin_cases i <;> simp [sachsFieldMatrix, sachsPhi1]

/-- The second column of the Sachs-Schwebel field matrix is `φ₂`. -/
lemma sachsFieldMatrix_col_one (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (fun i => sachsFieldMatrix E H i 1) = sachsPhi2 E H := by
  funext i
  fin_cases i <;> simp [sachsFieldMatrix, sachsPhi2]

/-- The determinant of the Sachs-Schwebel field matrix is the negative of the
Maxwell invariant. -/
lemma sachsFieldMatrix_det (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsFieldMatrix E H).det = -sachsMaxwellInvariant E H := by
  have hI2 : Complex.I ^ 2 = (-1 : ℂ) := by
    rw [pow_two, Complex.I_mul_I]
  unfold sachsFieldMatrix sachsMaxwellInvariant
  rw [Matrix.det_fin_two]
  simp [Fin.sum_univ_three]
  ring_nf
  rw [hI2]
  ring

/-- The trace of the Sachs-Schwebel field matrix is `2G₃`. -/
lemma sachsFieldMatrix_trace (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsFieldMatrix E H).trace = 2 * sachsRiemannSilbersteinField E H 2 := by
  unfold sachsFieldMatrix
  rw [Matrix.trace_fin_two]
  simp
  ring

/-- The scale factor `4πi` appearing in the Sachs-Schwebel source spinors. -/
noncomputable def sachsSourceScale : ℂ := ((4 * Real.pi : ℝ) : ℂ) * Complex.I

/-- The unscaled Sachs-Schwebel current matrix. Its determinant is the current
Minkowski norm `j²-ρ²`. -/
def sachsCurrentSpinorMatrix (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ :=
  !![((-ρ + j 2 : ℝ) : ℂ), ((-j 0 : ℝ) : ℂ) + Complex.I * ((j 1 : ℝ) : ℂ);
    ((j 0 : ℝ) : ℂ) + Complex.I * ((j 1 : ℝ) : ℂ), ((ρ + j 2 : ℝ) : ℂ)]

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (4), source matrix.** This is `4πi` times the
current spinor matrix. -/
noncomputable def sachsSourceMatrix (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ :=
  sachsSourceScale • sachsCurrentSpinorMatrix ρ j

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (4), first source spinor** `T₁`. -/
noncomputable def sachsSource1 (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Fin 2 → ℂ :=
  fun i => sachsSourceMatrix ρ j i 0

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (4), second source spinor** `T₂`. -/
noncomputable def sachsSource2 (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Fin 2 → ℂ :=
  fun i => sachsSourceMatrix ρ j i 1

/-- The current invariant `j²-ρ²` used by Sachs and Schwebel. -/
def sachsCurrentMinkowskiNorm (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) : ℝ :=
  sachsFieldNormSq j - ρ ^ 2

/-- The determinant of the unscaled source/current spinor matrix is `j²-ρ²`. -/
lemma sachsCurrentSpinorMatrix_det (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsCurrentSpinorMatrix ρ j).det = (sachsCurrentMinkowskiNorm ρ j : ℂ) := by
  have hI2 : Complex.I ^ 2 = (-1 : ℂ) := by
    rw [pow_two, Complex.I_mul_I]
  unfold sachsCurrentSpinorMatrix sachsCurrentMinkowskiNorm sachsFieldNormSq
  rw [Matrix.det_fin_two]
  simp [Fin.sum_univ_three]
  ring_nf
  rw [hI2]
  ring

/-- The source matrix columns recover the Sachs-Schwebel source spinors. -/
lemma sachsSourceMatrix_col_zero (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (fun i => sachsSourceMatrix ρ j i 0) = sachsSource1 ρ j := rfl

/-- The source matrix columns recover the second Sachs-Schwebel source spinor. -/
lemma sachsSourceMatrix_col_one (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (fun i => sachsSourceMatrix ρ j i 1) = sachsSource2 ρ j := rfl

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (5), four-component field spinor.** The upper
two components are `φ₁`; the lower two components are `φ₂`. -/
def sachsBispinor (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Fin 4 → ℂ :=
  ![sachsPhi1 E H 0, sachsPhi1 E H 1, sachsPhi2 E H 0, sachsPhi2 E H 1]

/-- The first component of the Sachs-Schwebel four-spinor is `φ₁₀`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsBispinor_zero (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsBispinor E H 0 = sachsPhi1 E H 0 := by
  simp [sachsBispinor]

/-- The second component of the Sachs-Schwebel four-spinor is `φ₁₁`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsBispinor_one (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsBispinor E H 1 = sachsPhi1 E H 1 := by
  simp [sachsBispinor]

/-- The third component of the Sachs-Schwebel four-spinor is `φ₂₀`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsBispinor_two (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsBispinor E H 2 = sachsPhi2 E H 0 := by
  simp [sachsBispinor]

/-- The fourth component of the Sachs-Schwebel four-spinor is `φ₂₁`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsBispinor_three (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsBispinor E H 3 = sachsPhi2 E H 1 := by
  simp [sachsBispinor]

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (5), four-component source spinor.** In the paper's
notation this is `T = (-iT₂, iT₁)`, with the two entries of each source spinor
listed consecutively. -/
noncomputable def sachsBispinorSource (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Fin 4 → ℂ :=
  ![-Complex.I * sachsSource2 ρ j 0, -Complex.I * sachsSource2 ρ j 1,
    Complex.I * sachsSource1 ρ j 0, Complex.I * sachsSource1 ρ j 1]

/-- The first component of the four-source is `-iT₂₀`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsBispinorSource_zero (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsBispinorSource ρ j 0 = -Complex.I * sachsSource2 ρ j 0 := by
  simp [sachsBispinorSource]

/-- The second component of the four-source is `-iT₂₁`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsBispinorSource_one (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsBispinorSource ρ j 1 = -Complex.I * sachsSource2 ρ j 1 := by
  simp [sachsBispinorSource]

/-- The third component of the four-source is `iT₁₀`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsBispinorSource_two (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsBispinorSource ρ j 2 = Complex.I * sachsSource1 ρ j 0 := by
  simp [sachsBispinorSource]

/-- The fourth component of the four-source is `iT₁₁`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsBispinorSource_three (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsBispinorSource ρ j 3 = Complex.I * sachsSource1 ρ j 1 := by
  simp [sachsBispinorSource]

/-- A first-order Sachs-Schwebel two-spinor operator. In Eq. (7), this is
`σ_μ ∂_μ`; the present definition keeps the differential operator abstract so
the algebraic equivalence with Eq. (9) is independent of coordinates. -/
abbrev SachsSpinorOperator := (Fin 2 → ℂ) → Fin 2 → ℂ

/-- Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (7), written for one two-spinor column. -/
def sachsColumnEquation (D : SachsSpinorOperator) (φ T : Fin 2 → ℂ) : Prop :=
  D φ = T

/-- Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (9), written as the columnwise `2×2` matrix equation
`D Φ = Σ`. -/
def sachsMatrixEquation
    (D : SachsSpinorOperator) (Φ Src : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) : Prop :=
  ∀ a, D (fun i => Φ i a) = fun i => Src i a

/-- Eq. (9) is exactly the two uncoupled Sachs-Schwebel equations Eq. (7) for
the two columns `φ₁` and `φ₂`. -/
lemma sachsMatrixEquation_iff_columns
    (D : SachsSpinorOperator) (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    sachsMatrixEquation D (sachsFieldMatrix E H) (sachsSourceMatrix ρ j) ↔
      sachsColumnEquation D (sachsPhi1 E H) (sachsSource1 ρ j) ∧
        sachsColumnEquation D (sachsPhi2 E H) (sachsSource2 ρ j) := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    constructor
    · have h0 := h 0
      simpa [sachsColumnEquation, sachsFieldMatrix_col_zero E H,
        sachsSourceMatrix_col_zero ρ j] using h0
    · have h1 := h 1
      simpa [sachsColumnEquation, sachsFieldMatrix_col_one E H,
        sachsSourceMatrix_col_one ρ j] using h1
  · intro h a
    fin_cases a
    · simpa [sachsColumnEquation, sachsFieldMatrix_col_zero E H,
        sachsSourceMatrix_col_zero ρ j] using h.1
    · simpa [sachsColumnEquation, sachsFieldMatrix_col_one E H,
        sachsSourceMatrix_col_one ρ j] using h.2

/-- **Wigner time reversal for two spinors**, `W = iσ₂K`. With the Pauli
convention in this file, `W(a,b) = (conj b, -conj a)`. -/
def sachsWignerTimeReverse (u : Fin 2 → ℂ) : Fin 2 → ℂ :=
  ![star (u 1), -star (u 0)]

/-- The first component of `W(a,b)` is `conj b`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsWignerTimeReverse_zero (u : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsWignerTimeReverse u 0 = star (u 1) := by
  simp [sachsWignerTimeReverse]

/-- The second component of `W(a,b)` is `-conj a`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsWignerTimeReverse_one (u : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsWignerTimeReverse u 1 = -star (u 0) := by
  simp [sachsWignerTimeReverse]

/-- The spin-`1/2` Wigner operator squares to `-1`: `W²u = -u`. -/
lemma sachsWignerTimeReverse_sq (u : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsWignerTimeReverse (sachsWignerTimeReverse u) = -u := by
  funext i
  fin_cases i <;> simp [sachsWignerTimeReverse]

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (8), Dirac-form field pair.** The first entry is
`φ₁`; the second entry is the Wigner-transformed `Wφ₂`. -/
def sachsDiracFieldPair (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : (Fin 2 → ℂ) × (Fin 2 → ℂ) :=
  (sachsPhi1 E H, sachsWignerTimeReverse (sachsPhi2 E H))

/-- The first Sachs-Schwebel Dirac-form field is `φ₁`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsDiracFieldPair_fst (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsDiracFieldPair E H).1 = sachsPhi1 E H := rfl

/-- The second Sachs-Schwebel Dirac-form field is `Wφ₂`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsDiracFieldPair_snd (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsDiracFieldPair E H).2 = sachsWignerTimeReverse (sachsPhi2 E H) := rfl

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (8), Dirac-form source pair.** The first entry is
`T₁`; the second entry is the Wigner-transformed `WT₂`. -/
noncomputable def sachsDiracSourcePair (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (Fin 2 → ℂ) × (Fin 2 → ℂ) :=
  (sachsSource1 ρ j, sachsWignerTimeReverse (sachsSource2 ρ j))

/-- The first Sachs-Schwebel Dirac-form source is `T₁`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsDiracSourcePair_fst (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsDiracSourcePair ρ j).1 = sachsSource1 ρ j := rfl

/-- The second Sachs-Schwebel Dirac-form source is `WT₂`. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsDiracSourcePair_snd (ρ : ℝ) (j : Fin 3 → ℝ) :
    (sachsDiracSourcePair ρ j).2 = sachsWignerTimeReverse (sachsSource2 ρ j) := rfl


/-! ## F. Poynting balance from the conservation-law section -/

/-- The Hermitian two-spinor scalar `u†v` used in Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (11). -/
def sachsHermitianPair (u v : Fin 2 → ℂ) : ℂ :=
  star (u 0) * v 0 + star (u 1) * v 1

/-- A zero second spinor gives zero Sachs-Schwebel Hermitian pairing. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsHermitianPair_zero_right (u : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsHermitianPair u 0 = 0 := by
  simp [sachsHermitianPair]

/-- A zero first spinor gives zero Sachs-Schwebel Hermitian pairing. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsHermitianPair_zero_left (v : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsHermitianPair 0 v = 0 := by
  simp [sachsHermitianPair]

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (11) source term for the bilinear conservation laws:
`φ_β†T_α + T_β†φ_α`, written for columns of the field and source matrices. -/
def sachsConservationSourceTerm
    (Φ Src : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) (α β : Fin 2) : ℂ :=
  sachsHermitianPair (fun i => Φ i β) (fun i => Src i α) +
    sachsHermitianPair (fun i => Src i β) (fun i => Φ i α)

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (11) conservation-law schema. `Div α β`
represents the divergence of the corresponding bilinear current. -/
def sachsConservationLaw
    (Div Φ Src : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) : Prop :=
  ∀ α β, Div α β = sachsConservationSourceTerm Φ Src α β

/-- In the source-free Sachs-Schwebel system, the Eq. (11) source term vanishes. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsConservationSourceTerm_zero_source
    (Φ : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) (α β : Fin 2) :
    sachsConservationSourceTerm Φ 0 α β = 0 := by
  unfold sachsConservationSourceTerm sachsHermitianPair
  simp

/-- With zero source matrix, the Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (11) conservation law is
equivalent to vanishing divergence matrix. -/
lemma sachsConservationLaw_zero_source_iff
    (Div Φ : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) :
    sachsConservationLaw Div Φ 0 ↔ Div = 0 := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    ext α β
    simpa [sachsConservationLaw] using h α β
  · intro h
    subst Div
    intro α β
    simp

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel electromagnetic energy density `(E²+H²)/(8π)`. -/
noncomputable def sachsEnergyDensity (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : ℝ :=
  (1 / (8 * Real.pi)) * (sachsFieldNormSq E + sachsFieldNormSq H)

/-- The unnormalised Poynting vector `E×H`; Eq. (12) uses `(1/4π) div(E×H)`. -/
def sachsPoyntingVector (E H : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Fin 3 → ℝ := crossProduct E H

/-- **Sachs-Schwebel Eq. (12), scalar balance form.**
`energyRate` stands for `∂ₜ(E²+H²)`, and `poyntingDiv` for `∇·(E×H)`. -/
noncomputable def sachsPoyntingBalance
    (energyRate poyntingDiv : ℝ) (E j : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (1 / (8 * Real.pi)) * energyRate + (1 / (4 * Real.pi)) * poyntingDiv = -sachsFieldDot E j

/-- Solving the Sachs-Schwebel Poynting balance for the energy-rate term. -/
lemma sachsPoyntingBalance_energyRate
    {energyRate poyntingDiv : ℝ} {E j : Fin 3 → ℝ}
    (h : sachsPoyntingBalance energyRate poyntingDiv E j) :
    energyRate = -2 * poyntingDiv - (8 * Real.pi) * sachsFieldDot E j := by
  unfold sachsPoyntingBalance at h
  have hmul := congrArg (fun x : ℝ => (8 * Real.pi) * x) h
  have hπ : Real.pi ≠ 0 := Real.pi_ne_zero
  field_simp [hπ] at hmul
  ring_nf at hmul ⊢
  linarith

/-- With no Poynting flux divergence and no electric work, the Sachs-Schwebel
energy-rate term vanishes. -/
lemma sachsPoyntingBalance_energyRate_zero
    {energyRate : ℝ} {E j : Fin 3 → ℝ}
    (h : sachsPoyntingBalance energyRate 0 E j) (hwork : sachsFieldDot E j = 0) :
    energyRate = 0 := by
  rw [sachsPoyntingBalance_energyRate h, hwork]
  ring


/-! ## G. Sachs-Schwebel appendix covariance through `SL(2,ℂ)` -/

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel proper-Lorentz spinor action `φ ↦ Sφ` on a two-spinor. -/
def sachsSL2CSpinorAction (S : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) (φ : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    Fin 2 → ℂ :=
  S *ᵥ φ

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel spinor action is ordinary matrix-vector multiplication. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsSL2CSpinorAction_apply
    (S : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) (φ : Fin 2 → ℂ) (i : Fin 2) :
    sachsSL2CSpinorAction S φ i = ∑ j, S i j * φ j := by
  fin_cases i <;> simp [sachsSL2CSpinorAction, Matrix.mulVec, Fin.sum_univ_two]

/-- The identity spin transformation leaves a Sachs-Schwebel two-spinor fixed. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsSL2CSpinorAction_one (φ : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsSL2CSpinorAction 1 φ = φ := by
  ext i
  simp [sachsSL2CSpinorAction]

/-- Composition of Sachs-Schwebel spin transformations is matrix multiplication. -/
lemma sachsSL2CSpinorAction_mul
    (S T : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ) (φ : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsSL2CSpinorAction (S * T) φ =
      sachsSL2CSpinorAction S (sachsSL2CSpinorAction T φ) := by
  simpa [sachsSL2CSpinorAction] using (Matrix.mulVec_mulVec φ S T).symm

/-- The Sachs-Schwebel source transformation from the appendix, `T ↦ (Sᵀ)⁻¹T`. -/
noncomputable def sachsSL2CSourceCovariantAction (S : SL(2, ℂ)) (T : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    Fin 2 → ℂ :=
  (S.1ᵀ)⁻¹ *ᵥ T

/-- The identity source transformation leaves a Sachs-Schwebel source spinor fixed. -/
@[simp] lemma sachsSL2CSourceCovariantAction_one (T : Fin 2 → ℂ) :
    sachsSL2CSourceCovariantAction (1 : SL(2, ℂ)) T = T := by
  ext i
  simp [sachsSL2CSourceCovariantAction]

/-- Sachs-Schwebel appendix covariance, linked to the repository's
`SL(2,ℂ) → SO⁺(1,3)` construction. The Pauli self-adjoint determinant is
preserved, and the induced Lorentz transformation is proper and orthochronous. -/
lemma sachsSL2CAppendixCovariance
    (S : SL(2, ℂ)) (A : selfAdjoint (Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ)) :
    Matrix.det ((Lorentz.SL2C.toSelfAdjointMap S) A).1 = Matrix.det A.1
      ∧ Matrix.det (Lorentz.SL2C.toLorentzGroup S).val = 1
      ∧ LorentzGroup.IsOrthochronous (Lorentz.SL2C.toLorentzGroup S) := by
  exact ⟨Lorentz.SL2C.toSelfAdjointMap_apply_det S A,
    Lorentz.SL2C.toLorentzGroup_det_one S,
    Lorentz.SL2C.toLorentzGroup_isOrthochronous S⟩

end Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.Electromagnetic.EMFieldSuperoperator

